# the argument of meno-paradox.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Socrates only reports Meno's argument ("You argue ..."); the text does not assert it in its own voice.
# The main conclusion is Meno's: a man cannot enquire about what he knows, nor about what he does not know.
atom	need_known	a man who knows a thing has need to enquire about it
atom	enquire_known	a man can enquire about that which he knows
atom	knows_subject	the man knows the very subject about which he is to enquire
atom	enquire_unknown	a man can enquire about that which he does not know
P1	S2	¬need_known	says meno
P2	S2	¬knows_subject → ¬enquire_unknown	says meno
P3	S2	¬knows_subject	says meno
P4	S2	¬enquire_known ∧ ¬enquire_unknown	says meno
# Unstated step Meno's argument relies on: one cannot enquire into what one has no need to enquire into.
A1	-	¬need_known → ¬enquire_known
# "cannot" is read as plain impossibility of enquiry, not as a modal operator.
# C restates P4, Meno's conclusion. Its premises (P1, P2, P3) are attributed only, so the text never draws it in its own voice.
C	S2	¬enquire_known ∧ ¬enquire_unknown
